WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:04.870
MRS.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.870
OBAMA: Thanks for being here.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:06.870
You guys, as I always say, rest yourselves.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:07.870
You got a lot of work to do.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:08.870
(Laughter.)

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.950
Well, welcome to the White House.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.460
It is truly a pleasure to be here with all
of you today for the first ever White House

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.220
Convening on Food Marketing to Children.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:25.880
But before we get started, I just wanted to
take a moment to express my sincere heartbreak

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:32.040
over Monday's tragedy at the Navy Yard here
in Washington.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:39.539
The men and women who lost their lives devoted
their careers to protecting our country.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:45.949
And as we mourn those that we lost and we
pray for those who were injured, we also honor

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:51.969
their service, and we hold all of their loved
ones in our hearts at this very difficult

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:54.120
time.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.869
So with that, we're going to put you to work.

00:00:57.869 --> 00:00:59.600
Thank you again for being here.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.250
I want to start by thanking Sam for that very
kind introduction, but more importantly for

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:12.170
all the work that he and his team have done
to make Let's Move a reality.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.340
And I want to thank all of you for joining
us today.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:21.280
All of you in this room, you come to this
issue from all different angles.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.500
You're experts, advocates, parents.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.391
You represent food and beverage companies,
media and entertainment companies, and so

00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:30.680
much more.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.920
And we're eager to have a lively and constructive
dialogue with you about how we market food

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.460
to our children.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:42.920
We're eager to hear more from everyone in
this room about what's working, where we're

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:49.770
falling short, and how we can keep moving
forward together on this complex and challenging

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.770
but very important issue.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.670
And I think it's important to note that we're
having this conversation in the midst of what

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:03.610
I believe is a cultural shift that is happening
in this country -- a transformation in how

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:11.750
we live and eat that many of us could never
have imagined even just a few years ago.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.040
I see it everywhere I go all across this country.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:20.819
I see it in chain restaurants that are serving
kale salads, and they're filling kids' menus

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:26.629
with not just nuggets and fries, but with
broccoli and whole-wheat pasta.

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:32.340
I see it in churches where instead of fried
chicken and mac and cheese for church supper,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.080
they're serving up grilled fish and brown
rice.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.260
I see it online where parenting, cooking,
and health blogs are crammed with healthy

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:48.040
recipes and tips about providing better nutrition
for our kids.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:54.760
And I see it in schools where students can't
wait to tell me about their new salad bar

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:01.450
or how they ate a radish or tried cauliflower
for the first time, and actually like it.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:07.329
In fact, we recently sponsored a recipe contest
for kids here at the White House, and we got

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:14.709
over 1,300 entries from 50 states across the
country, and I can't tell you how many of

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:17.450
those recipes featured quinoa.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.489
(Laughter.)

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:20.560
Go figure.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.040
And if all of this doesn't provide sufficient
evidence of an important shift, then just

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.680
take a look at the market research.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.499
The National Restaurant Association surveyed
chefs about trends in their industry, and

00:03:33.499 --> 00:03:39.150
three of the top 10 trends were specifically
about healthy kids' meals.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:46.079
A survey by the Food Marketing Institute found
that 90 percent of grocery stores are offering

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:51.599
healthy recipes to their customers, and 98
percent of them maintain health and wellness

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:52.769
websites.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:59.059
And today, for the first time in decades,
we're actually starting to move the needle

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:01.090
on this issue.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:08.439
Between 2008 and 2011, obesity rates among
low-income preschoolers dropped in 19 states

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:11.349
and territories across the country.

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:16.580
And childhood obesity rates are falling in
cities like New York and Philadelphia, and

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.090
in states like California and Mississippi.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:28.139
But while we have made important progress,
when one in three kids is still on track to

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:36.710
develop diabetes, and when diet has now surpassed
smoking as the number one risk factor for

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:42.319
disease and death in this country, then we
clearly have much more work to do.

00:04:42.319 --> 00:04:48.690
And, yes, we have made meaningful changes
in a number of areas by getting healthier

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:54.259
food into our schools and communities, but
at the end of the day, if we truly want to

00:04:54.259 --> 00:05:00.490
solve this problem, we also need to get our
kids to actually want to eat these healthier

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:01.669
options.

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:07.030
And I say this not just as a First Lady who's
been working on this issue for the past three

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:14.560
and a half years; I say this as a mom who
has been working hard to raise two girls.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.050
As you know, I haven't always lived in the
White House.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.050
(Laughter.)

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:24.510
Not so long ago, I was a busy working mom
desperate to find quick, affordable meals

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.750
and snacks for my family.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:30.190
I needed cereal that my girls could pour themselves.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.520
I needed lunches I could pack in a hurry.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:37.539
I needed juice boxes that my three-year-old
could hold in her hand in the backseat of

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:38.760
my car.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:44.199
I needed dinners that came pre-cooked and
ready to pop in the microwave.

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:49.300
And most importantly, I needed my kids to
actually eat all of this food I was buying

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.949
for them.

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:56.490
And all of you in the food and beverage industry
delivered for me.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:02.150
You manufactured and sold the convenience
foods I needed, and you did a brilliant job

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.020
making those foods something my kids would
want.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:12.419
So when I opened those boxes and bags, my
girls were happy, and I was happy -- problem

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:13.419
solved.

00:06:13.419 --> 00:06:19.750
But then, like a lot of moms, I started learning
more about nutrition and health.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.629
I started reading labels.

00:06:22.629 --> 00:06:26.350
I started getting warnings from my pediatrician
about the health of my

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.229
Kids.

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:34.460
And I began to realize that some of these
foods that were so quick and cheap and tasty

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.670
weren't always healthy for my kids.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:41.330
So once again, moms like me turned to your
industry for help.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:45.789
But this time, we didn't just want the foods
to be convenient and affordable -- we wanted

00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:48.199
them to be good for our kids as well.

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:53.870
And once again, many of you have started to
deliver by manufacturing some of the healthier

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:55.250
options.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:58.310
And that is an important first step.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:05.889
But once again, moms like me are relying on
all of you to actually help our kids get excited

00:07:05.889 --> 00:07:08.460
about eating that food.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:15.090
And that's why I wanted to bring all of you
together today -- because you guys know better

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:18.159
than anyone how to get kids excited.

00:07:18.159 --> 00:07:22.889
You've done it before, and we need you to
do it again.

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:28.509
And fortunately you have everything it takes
to get this done because through the magic

00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:33.389
of marketing and advertising, all of you,
more than anyone else, have the power to shape

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:36.909
our kids' tastes and desires.

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:41.379
You all know that our kids are like little
sponges --- they absorb whatever is around

00:07:41.379 --> 00:07:42.379
them.

00:07:42.379 --> 00:07:46.919
But they don't yet have the ability to question
and analyze what they're told.

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:53.650
Instead, they believe just about everything
they see and hear, especially if it's on TV.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:59.530
And when the average child is now spending
nearly eight hours a day in front of some

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:05.610
kind of screen, many of their opinions and
preferences are being shaped by the marketing

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.330
campaigns you all create.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.389
And that's where the problem comes in.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:17.689
You see, the average child watches thousands
of food advertisements each year, and 86 percent

00:08:17.689 --> 00:08:23.719
of these ads are for products loaded with
sugar, fat, salt.

00:08:23.719 --> 00:08:31.220
By contrast, our kids see an average of just
one ad a week for healthy products like water

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.360
to fruits and vegetables.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.190
Just one ad a week.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.099
And as you all know, these ads work.

00:08:38.099 --> 00:08:44.560
Kids who see foods advertised on TV are significantly
more likely to ask for them at the store --- a

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.010
phenomenon known as "pester power."

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.010
(Laughter.)

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.010
New to me.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.740
Sounds right though.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:59.320
And research shows that a child's first request
for a product happens as early as 24 months,

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:05.230
and 75 percent of the time, this request takes
place in a grocery store.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:08.840
And given what our kids are seeing on TV,
it should come as no surprise what they're

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.680
asking for.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:16.290
One study revealed that 45 percent of kids'
food requests were things like cookies and

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:22.210
candy, burgers and fries, and chips, but just
3 percent were for fruits and vegetables.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:28.329
So from the time our kids are still in diapers,
we as parents are already fighting an uphill

00:09:28.329 --> 00:09:29.329
battle to

00:09:29.329 --> 00:09:35.250
get them interested in the foods that will
actually nourish them and help them grow.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:40.850
Now, like many parents, Barack and I do our
best to limit our daughters' TV time.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.120
But as you all know, these ads aren't just
on TV.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.250
They're on the internet, in video games, smart
phones, billboards.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:50.220
They're in schools and store displays.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:56.620
They're everywhere, and parents just can't
keep up, no matter how hard we try.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:02.060
So whatever we all might believe about personal
responsibility and self-determination, I think

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.060
we

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.040
can agree that it doesn't necessarily apply
to children.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:12.310
I think that we can all agree that parents
deserve more control over the products and

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.930
messages their kids are exposed to.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:21.830
And that's why I was so pleased that 17 major
American companies came together on their

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:27.250
own as part of the Children's Food and Beverage
Advertising Initiative to adopt new standards

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:28.720
for marketing to kids.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:30.670
And I know this wasn't easy.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:37.570
Forging consensus among fierce competitors
is a challenge to say the least.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:43.320
But these new standards are beginning to have
an impact, and I commend all of these companies

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.790
for taking action.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:52.639
But of course, while limiting the marketing
of unhealthy food is critical, it's not enough.

00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:59.130
We also need companies to actually market
healthy foods to kids --- foods that have

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:00.130
real

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:05.089
nutritional value, foods that are fortified
with real fruits and vegetables, whole grains

00:11:05.089 --> 00:11:06.520
and low-fat dairy.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:13.970
Now, I say this mindful that companies exist
to make a profit, and they need that in order

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:14.990
to survive.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:18.610
And those profits keep our economy going every
day.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:23.730
But the fact is that marketing nutritious
foods to our kids isn't just good for our

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.420
kids' health -- it can also be good for companies'
bottom lines.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:35.480
For example, the folks at Birds Eye Vegetables
launched a major marketing campaign featuring

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.550
characters from the popular kids' show "iCarly."

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:44.350
And their sales jumped 20 percent in just
two months.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:49.250
Vidalia Onion did a campaign with "Shrek"
-- one of my favorites -- and their sales

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.209
went up 50 percent.

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:58.770
So I'm confident that you all can sell healthy
foods to our kids and remain competitive and

00:11:58.770 --> 00:11:59.970
profitable.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:05.380
That's why some of your companies' marketers
are playing a key role in a new campaign to

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.339
inspire people in this country to drink more
water.

00:12:09.339 --> 00:12:15.130
You see, we know that water is such an important
component to good health, but so many other

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:21.110
beverages have millions of marketing dollars
behind them that water often gets drowned

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.320
out.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.860
That's why last week, we launched the new
Drink Up campaign -- a campaign to bring attention

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.460
to water as a healthy choice so that when
people are thirsty, they reach for a glass

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:33.779
of water.

00:12:33.779 --> 00:12:38.480
So I say this because when it comes to believing
in the power of marketing to promote healthy

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.490
choices to our kids, I'm not just talking
the talk, I'm actually walking the walk on

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:45.520
this one.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.769
But so far, I've spent a lot of time talking
about food and beverage companies.

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:55.690
But those of you from media companies also
play a critical role in marketing food to

00:12:55.690 --> 00:13:00.889
our children, and I want to call on all of
you to do your part as well.

00:13:00.889 --> 00:13:06.940
That means, for example, limiting the use
of your licensed characters to market unhealthy

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:12.250
food to kids, and limiting unhealthy food
ads in your programming.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:17.420
Disney has pledged to do just that, and I
know that other media companies can follow

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:18.589
suit.

00:13:18.589 --> 00:13:24.259
But I'm also asking you to actually use your
licensed characters to promote healthy food.

00:13:24.259 --> 00:13:30.019
"Sesame Street" has been doing this for years,
which is why, when parents turn on "Sesame

00:13:30.019 --> 00:13:36.600
Street" or the Disney Channel, they know and
trust that their kids won't be bombarded with

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.380
messages promoting unhealthy food.

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:42.720
And that trust is valuable.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.569
That trust is good for your businesses.

00:13:45.569 --> 00:13:49.949
That's why so many of you in the private sector
are leading the way on this issue.

00:13:49.949 --> 00:13:51.540
You are innovating.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.209
You're taking risks.

00:13:53.209 --> 00:13:58.580
You're serving as models for your industries,
and it's starting to make a difference -- not

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:02.190
just for our kids, but for your shareholders
as well.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:09.350
And I'm here today with one simple request
-- and that is to do even more and move even

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:13.529
faster to market responsibly to our kids.

00:14:13.529 --> 00:14:16.940
Now, I want to be clear about what I'm asking
here.

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:22.290
I am not asking anyone to take the fun out
of childhood.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:27.329
As we all know, treats are one of the best
parts of being a kid.

00:14:27.329 --> 00:14:32.490
Instead, the goal here is to empower parents
instead of undermining them as they try to

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:35.740
make healthier choices for their families.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:41.430
And we need you to lead the way in creating
demand for healthy foods so that kids actually

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:46.269
start "pestering" us for those foods in the
grocery store.

00:14:46.269 --> 00:14:50.420
And then parents actually start buying them,
and then companies have incentives to make

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:52.860
and sell even more of those foods.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:58.959
And ideally, in a decade or so, we would see
a dramatic shift across the entire industry.

00:14:58.959 --> 00:15:04.139
We'd see companies shifting marketing dollars
away from those less healthy products and

00:15:04.139 --> 00:15:08.730
investing those dollars in your healthier
products instead.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:14.880
That's how we can make healthy eating a way
of life for our kids and for our families.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:18.630
And make no mistake about it, meeting these
goals isn't just the right decision for the

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:24.009
short or medium term; it can affect your businesses
for decades to come.

00:15:24.009 --> 00:15:28.080
See, the decisions that you make about marketing
won't just affect what our kids are eating

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:34.230
today -- those decisions are going to also
affect the health of your workforce tomorrow.

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:40.490
Businesses are losing $37 [$73] billion a
year due to absenteeism, lost productivity,

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:45.769
and health care costs associated with obesity-related
conditions.

00:15:45.769 --> 00:15:51.560
And just imagine what that number will look
like in twenty years from now if we stand

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:57.459
by and let today's unhealthy kids grow into
unhealthy adults who become the employees

00:15:57.459 --> 00:15:59.670
of tomorrow.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:04.819
And there's another long-term factor that's
also worth considering, and that is the potential

00:16:04.819 --> 00:16:08.690
trend in our kids' food preferences and taste.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:14.610
You see, over the past few years, we've seen
some real changes in the foods our kids are

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:15.610
eating,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.639
starting from the time they're born.

00:16:17.639 --> 00:16:22.779
For example, changes in the Women, Infants
and Children Program are helping millions

00:16:22.779 --> 00:16:26.430
of women across America buy healthier products
for their kids.

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:32.660
And 10,000 childcare centers across the country
have committed to serving more nutritious

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:35.680
food as part of Let's Move Childcare.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:41.069
In addition, we have implemented sweeping
changes in America's school lunch program,

00:16:41.069 --> 00:16:46.170
such that nearly 32 million children across
the country are now eating more fruits, and

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:50.230
vegetables, whole grains, and other healthy
foods every day.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:56.649
And starting next year, school vending machines
will be stocked with healthy items as well.

00:16:56.649 --> 00:17:02.660
So healthier eating is starting to become
the new norm for our kids.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:08.430
This is what they're getting used to, and
for many, this is all they'll ever know.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:13.959
And as their palates and their habits adjust,
that could have a serious effect on their

00:17:13.959 --> 00:17:17.799
taste and preferences not just as children,
but for the rest of their lives.

00:17:17.799 --> 00:17:24.560
It could even affect what they ultimately
buy and serve their own children in the future.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:29.700
So this isn't just some passing trend or fad.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:35.600
So there might be those out there whose strategy
is to just wait this out -- folks who might

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:42.090
still be thinking to themselves, well, in
a few years, this lady will be gone -- (laughter)

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:46.590
-- and this whole Let's Move thing will finally
be over, so we can go back to business as

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:47.590
usual.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:50.400
And I know that none of you here are thinking
that way.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:51.400
(Laughter.)

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:56.890
But if you know anyone who is -- (laughter)
-- you might want to remind them that I didn't

00:17:56.890 --> 00:18:01.789
create this issue, and it's not going to go
away three and a half years from now when

00:18:01.789 --> 00:18:04.100
I'm no longer First Lady.

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:10.280
This issue has truly taken on a life of its
own because it is affecting just about every

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:14.070
family and every community in this country.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:21.539
Parents are increasingly anxious as they see
their kids developing diabetes, high cholesterol,

00:18:21.539 --> 00:18:26.080
high blood pressure -- conditions that would
have been unthinkable to find in children

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.280
just a generation ago.

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:35.350
There are now 79 million pre-diabetics in
this county, many of them young adults, and

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:36.350
we're

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:42.100
even seeing people in their twenties and thirties
having heart attacks and strokes.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:47.370
So I have yet to meet a single parent who
doesn't understand the threat of obesity to

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.210
their health and to their children's health.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:52.890
And they are looking for solutions.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:58.130
They're starting to think about how they feed
themselves and their families.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:02.270
And you all can take advantage of this.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.830
You can get ahead of the curve.

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:10.620
You have everything it takes right now to
seize this societal challenge as an unprecedented

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.330
business opportunity.

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:16.809
Right now, your companies employ some of the
most brilliant, creative, innovative minds

00:19:16.809 --> 00:19:19.350
in the entire country.

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:22.960
And you all can sell just about anything to
our kids.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:29.000
And if anyone can get our kids to eat their
vegetables, it's all of you.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:30.640
And I know it won't be easy.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:36.740
I know you might have to take some short-term
risks to get a long-term payoff.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:43.150
But that's what great American companies do
-- they act boldly, they innovate, they take

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:44.150
risks.

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:50.290
And remember, it wasn't that long ago that
"going green" or taking your business online

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:53.030
were considered risky endeavors.

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:57.380
But throughout our history, the companies
that saw where the future was headed and took

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:01.640
that leap have been rewarded.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:07.070
And I want you to know that I will do everything
in my power to celebrate and highlight this

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:10.130
kind of leadership on behalf of our kids.

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:13.409
That's what I've been doing since I first
started working on this issue, whether it

00:20:13.409 --> 00:20:14.690
was visiting a

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:20.730
Walmart stocked with fresh produce, or having
dinner at an Olive Garden with a healthier

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:27.360
kids menu, or hanging out with Mickey to celebrate
Disney's achievements on this issue.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:33.390
And I am eager to have these kinds of celebrations
with every company in this room.

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:36.700
So I hope that all of you will really engage.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:41.460
I hope that you'll really talk to each other,
and learn from each other, and come up with

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:45.090
new solutions that will make a real difference
for our kids.

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:49.549
We want to hear from everyone involved in
this issue -- from industry leaders to advocates

00:20:49.549 --> 00:20:53.610
to researchers and to parents -- because we're
all in this together.

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:57.700
And while I know I've been talking a lot about
corporate America's responsibilities on this

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:02.600
issue, the advocates and experts here today
have an important responsibility too.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.549
Your words matter.

00:21:04.549 --> 00:21:11.039
You all can help either provide incentives
to change, or you can be barriers to change.

00:21:11.039 --> 00:21:15.610
So we need you to be constructive in your
criticisms and strategic in your calls to

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:21.090
action, because when it comes to marketing,
it can be hard for companies to take risks.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:22.600
They face pressures from Wall Street.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:27.080
There are also limits to how fast they can
move and how far they can go before they start

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:28.710
losing customers.

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:34.210
So when companies do step up and take risks,
we need to be supportive, even if we think

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:36.310
they haven't gone far enough.

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:41.789
We need to help them make those risks pay
off, so that they'll go even farther, and

00:21:41.789 --> 00:21:45.200
so that other companies will follow their
lead.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:49.771
And as for those of you in the private sector,
I hope that you will head back to your companies

00:21:49.771 --> 00:21:57.169
ready to think big and act boldly on behalf
of our kids because while running a profitable

00:21:57.169 --> 00:22:03.950
business is important, I know that you all
aren't just business executives.

00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:10.270
You all are also good neighbors, and good
citizens, and proud parents and grandparents.

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:14.900
Many of you didn't go into business just to
make money, but to problem solve and make

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.300
people's lives better.

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.850
You went into business to create great American
products and build great American institutions,

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:26.990
and to leave a legacy that your kids and grandkids
will inherit with pride.

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:30.350
And in the end, that's what Let's Move is
all about.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:36.460
It's about the legacy we're leaving for the
next generation and generations to come.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:42.550
And standing here today with all of you, people
who represent some of the most visionary,

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:47.690
pioneering companies and organizations in
this country, I have never felt more confident

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:51.970
about our children's future and, of course,
the future of this country.

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:57.900
So I look forward to hearing about what you
all come up with today, and I look forward

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.320
to celebrating new commitments and achievements
in the months and years ahead.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:03.320
Thank you all.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:04.640
God bless you.

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:06.450
Good luck and work hard.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:06.570
(Applause.)

